🪤 **F-1675-1 (s1675 2026-08-11, MEASURED ON THE SEVENTH CONSECUTIVE HELD FIRE WHILE PRE-FLIGHTING THE DRAIN ITS FOUR PREDECESSORS PRESCRIBED — THE PENDING LANE-A DRAIN NEEDS A FRESHNESS PRE-CONDITION, BECAUSE BOTH OF ITS FAILURE MODES ARE SILENT AND THE WORSE ONE REVERTS MAIN. NOT AN OWNER QUESTION; DOES NOT JOIN THE DESK.)** 📋 **HOW IT SURFACED:** the board was dry and held for the seventh fire running, so — following the s1672 precedent of spending a held budget on the drain it is forbidden to perform — I asked the one question four next-fire instructions never asked: *when lane-a frees, will `git checkout lane/a -- <paths>` actually find anything there?* 📐 **MEASURED, read-only git plumbing, zero load on the running lane:** `lane/a`'s tip is **`031b61ff4` (2026-08-11T13:36:40)** — which is **s1657's own handoff commit**, not a runner commit: `ahead=0`, an **ancestor of main**, **91 behind**. **After 8h20m the 13:35 run has committed NOTHING.** And both deliverables are **byte-identical on lane/a and main** (`e30c810d…` 139,159 B · `51399654…` 2,358,427 B), so `git diff main lane/a -- logs/suite-red-inventory.md logs/suite-red-inventory-compact.json` is **EMPTY**. 🪤 **MODE 1 — SILENT NO-OP (what the recipe does TODAY):** the prescribed `git checkout lane/a -- <the two paths>` returns **rc=0, no output, zero diff**, i.e. a Silent No-Op (Mistake #1) wearing a successful path-scoped drain's costume — and the recipe's very next step flips the leaf `queued`→`merged` with a 40-char `mergeHash`, **recording a merge of nothing**. ⚠️ **MODE 2 — SILENT REVERT OF MAIN, worse and reachable:** proved by manufacturing it in a throwaway `/tmp` repo — when the ancestor's blob DIFFERS, `git checkout <ancestor-branch> -- path` **stages the OLDER content** (`M f.txt`), rc=0, no output: a regression of main dressed as a drain. **`lane/a` is 91 commits behind main, so only the accident that none of those 91 touched these two paths keeps us in mode 1 rather than mode 2.** 🔍 **WHY THE EXISTING GUARD IS STRUCTURALLY BLIND:** the recipe gates on `lane-usable.mjs lane-a` no longer saying **BUSY** — but **BUSY is a WORKTREE property (dirt + lock), while "the runner committed its output" is a COMMIT property.** The runner auto-commits at run **END**, so a runner that dies mid-run yields exactly *not-BUSY + nothing committed* — **and it has already died mid-run once today** (s1657, verbatim: *"the runner lost a third run mid-fire and was restarted (77853)"*). ✅ **CURE — ONE COMMAND, ADDITIVE, ZERO COST: before the checkout, require a real diff.** `git diff --quiet main lane/a -- logs/suite-red-inventory.md logs/suite-red-inventory-compact.json`. ⚠️ **READ THE SENSE, IT LOOKS INVERTED: `--quiet` exits 0 when there is NO diff** — so **rc=0 = STOP** (the run committed nothing; do not check out, do not flip the leaf, report it) and **rc=1 = real content, proceed**. A fire reading the usual "rc=0 = good" inverts the guard and ships the no-op. 💡 **REUSABLE HALF — WHEN YOU REPLACE AN INSTRUMENT FOR A GOOD REASON, INVENTORY THE SAFETY PROPERTIES THE OLD ONE GAVE YOU INCIDENTALLY.** Measured in the same probe: `git merge --no-ff <ancestor-branch>` announces **"Already up to date."** and creates no commit — it *refuses out loud*. **F-1672-1's swap to a path-scoped checkout is RIGHT on its own terms and its 150.8 MiB backup argument stands entirely untouched** — but the instrument it replaced carried a free no-op alarm, and the replacement is silent by construction. **The dropped property was never anyone's decision; it left with the tool.**
🩺 **F-1673-1 (s1673 2026-08-11, MEASURED ON THE FIFTH CONSECUTIVE HELD FIRE — THE CANONICAL "IS A BUSY LANE ALIVE?" INSTRUMENT IS THE IN-FLIGHT RUN'S OWN LOG, AND IT NEEDS NO PREDECESSOR'S BASELINE. NOT AN OWNER QUESTION; DOES NOT JOIN THE DESK.):** Five fires in a row (s1669–s1673) have re-answered the same question — *is lane-a still working?* — and each used a weaker proxy than the one sitting in `tasks/runs/`. s1672's method was a **tracked-dirt delta** (`lane-usable` said 1213 → 1215, "advancing", "it cost one command"). It is correct and it was the right call, but it has two structural limits worth writing down before a sixth fire inherits it: **(1) it is a DELTA, so it requires the PREDECESSOR to have recorded a baseline** — had s1671 not written 1213 into its handoff, s1672 had no answer at all and would have had to hold on faith; **(2) its granularity is a fire-interval (~10 min), and it cannot distinguish "wrote two screenshots then wedged" from "churning".** ➡️ **THE DIRECT INSTRUMENT, SELF-CONTAINED, TWO COMMANDS, NO BASELINE: the run's own log.** `ls -la tasks/runs/<stamp>-<lane>-<task>.md.log` → **mtime vs now answers ALIVE** (measured s1673: mtime **21:29**, now **21:30** — written inside the last minute). `grep -n "Serial progress" <same log> | tail` → **the run's SELF-REPORTED progress ladder answers ADVANCING** (measured: **188 → 216 → 244 → 255 → 295 → 325 of 1,706; 83 failures observed** — monotonic across six samples). One file, both halves of the question, and it is the implementer's own output stream rather than a side-effect of it. ⚠️ **AND THE TWO HALVES ARE BOTH REQUIRED — MTIME ALONE IS NOT SUFFICIENT, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS NOT MERELY "USE A BETTER FILE".** The same log carries a recurring `ERROR codex_models_manager::manager: failed to renew cache TTL: missing field \`base_instructions\`` roughly every **15 s** (observed at `14:29:44Z` and `14:29:59Z`, i.e. 21:29 local). A wedged run emitting that loop would **tick mtime forever** while accomplishing nothing — the exact instrument-that-lies shape the corpus is full of. **mtime proves the STREAM moved; the progress ladder proves WORK moved.** Ask both. ✅ **NEGATIVE RESULT, RECORDED SO NOBODY RE-DERIVES IT: the dashboard's run clock is HONEST and may be trusted.** `logs/task-stats.jsonl`'s `minutes` field (seen ticking **459 → 469** for `lane-a-f1643-2-suite-red-inventory-refresh` in this fire's own regen churn) is derived at `scripts/dashboard-gen.sh:124` as `int((mt0-se0)/60)` where `mt0 = os.path.getmtime(<run log>)` — **run-log mtime minus the filename stamp, NOT `now` minus the stamp.** It therefore FREEZES on a hung run rather than ticking, and is a genuine liveness signal. The opposite would have been a classic false clock; it was checked by reading the generator, not assumed. 📐 **THIRD SIGNAL, ALSO VALID, HIGHEST CONFIDENCE AND HIGHEST COST: the process tree.** `ps` filtered to `ms-playwright/chromium-*` shows **fresh** browser children with real CPU (s1673: pid `63729` **30.2%** at **1m38s** old, pid `64945` **42.6%** at **29s** old) — a per-spec spawn cadence, which is what "churning" physically looks like. Use it when the log is ambiguous; the log is cheaper and was sufficient here. 🚫 **DO NOT READ THE PROGRESS FIGURE AS A STALL SIGNAL, AND DO NOT ESCALATE THE DURATION — IT IS OWNER-SANCTIONED.** `325/1,706` after ~7h50m looks alarming and is not: the **THROTTLE LAW** (owner ruling 2026-08-11, verbatim *"can we limit the amount of threads it uses? I was just surprised it took so long. I woke up in the middle of the night to it."*) re-queued this very master with **`--workers=3` + `nice -n 19`**, explicitly buying a **"day-scale quiet hum"** in place of 8-worker saturation. The owner traded wall-time for quiet **on purpose**; a fire that discovers the slowness and raises it is re-litigating a settled ruling. ⚖️ **NO ETA IS DERIVED HERE AND NONE SHOULD BE** — F-1666-1 and F-1670-1 forbid it, `lane-usable.mjs` is self-enforcing, and the progress ladder is reported as a *phase fact*, not as an input to arithmetic. 💡 **REUSABLE HALF, PAST RUNNERS: WHEN AN AGENT REPORTS ON ITSELF, PREFER ITS OWN OUTPUT STREAM TO THE SIDE-EFFECTS YOU CAN OBSERVE FROM OUTSIDE.** The factory watched the *filesystem it dirtied* for five fires while the run was narrating its own position, by count, in a file the fire already knew the path of. **A proxy that needs a predecessor's baseline is a proxy that fails exactly when the chain of predecessors breaks** — which is the moment you most need the answer.
📦 **F-1672-1 (s1672 2026-08-11, MEASURED ON A HELD BOARD WHILE PRE-CLASSIFYING A DRAIN THAT HAS NOT HAPPENED YET — THE PENDING LANE-A DRAIN MUST BE PATH-SCOPED, BECAUSE THE `merge --no-ff` THAT §3.0b PRESCRIBES WOULD PUT A 150.8 MiB FILE ON MAIN AND PERMANENTLY BREAK THE ORIGIN BACKUP. NOT AN OWNER QUESTION.)** 📋 **BOARD:** dry and held — `find tasks/queue -type f` → **0** · lane-a **BUSY** (tracked-dirt **1213 → 1215** across this fire, i.e. advancing) · lanes b/c/d `ahead=0` USABLE and idle · no `tasks/CODEX-WALL` · assayer `pending/` EMPTY. Branch (B) applied and **the hold’s grounds were NOT re-derived a fourth time** — F-1670-1 forbids it and is right. So the budget went to the one thing a held fire can do for the drain it is forbidden to perform: **pre-classify its scope before the lane frees.** 🎯 **THE FINDING — FOUR FACTS, EACH MEASURED THIS FIRE, NONE INHERITED.** **(1)** `lane-runner-v3.sh:284` auto-commits every non-ART lane with `git add -A -- . ’:(exclude).wrangler’ ’:(exclude)logs/factory-usage.json’ ’:(exclude)logs/usage-history.jsonl’` — **three exclusions and nothing else**, so whatever is dirty in that worktree when the task ends lands on `lane/a`. **(2)** `worktrees/lane-a/logs/suite-red-inventory-raw.json` is **158,154,953 bytes (150.8 MiB)**; `git check-ignore` → **rc=1, NOT ignored**; `git ls-files --error-unmatch` → **fails, NOT tracked** ⇒ the sweep **will** stage it. And it is still growing — that is the **parallel** arm’s output alone (mtime 20:10 **local**), and scope 5’s serial arm rewrites it. **(3)** **GitHub hard-rejects any single file over 100 MiB on push**, and the backup pipeline is **live right now**, not dormant: `origin/main` = `0ad741512`, main exactly 1 ahead. **(4)** Recovery from that rejection needs a history rewrite plus a force-push — **force is deny-listed and rewriting ratified history is forbidden (CLAUDE.md §7.7)**. So the failure mode is not “a red push”, it is **a backup that no act available to this factory can repair.** ⚖️ **THE ASYMMETRY THAT SETTLES IT WITHOUT ANY CLEVERNESS.** Path-scoping costs **nothing**: the raw report stays on disk in the lane worktree, which is **exactly what the master asks** — its Touch-ONLY line reads *“(untracked; **leave it on disk, do not delete it** — RETENTION LAW, and it is the only provenance the next refresh will have)”*. Merging it costs the backup **permanently**. **One direction is reversible and the other is not**, so the drain fire does not need to weigh anything — only to scope its pathspec. ➡️ **BINDING FOR THE DRAIN FIRE:** do **NOT** `git merge --no-ff lane/a`. Take the deliverable by path — `git checkout lane/a -- logs/suite-red-inventory.md logs/suite-red-inventory-compact.json` — and commit those two paths, **checkout and commit as ONE act**. Do **not** push `lane/a` to origin either; the BACKUP LAW’s weekly lane-branch push hits the identical wall. ⚠️ **THIS CONTRADICTS A STANDING LAW ON PURPOSE, SO IT IS NAMED HERE RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE DISCOVERED MID-DRAIN.** §3.0b/F-1589-5 rules that a lane drain must `git merge --no-ff <branch> -m "…"` as ONE act and never `--no-commit` followed by review-writing — and **it is right**: it closes the window where a concurrent sweep commits a staged merge, measured twice (F-1295-1 working-tree side, F-1589-5 index side). **Its INTENT is “never leave undecided content staged”, and a path-scoped checkout+commit honours that intent exactly** while declining the instrument. F-1589-5 was priced against lanes whose diff is source; it was never priced against a lane whose diff is **1.55 GB of regenerated evidence.** 📊 **SCALE, for the drain fire’s planning:** the sweep is **1249 paths / 1554.2 MB** — **1215** modified tracked PNGs (**1370.1 MB**) plus **34** untracked files (**184.0 MB**). **The deliverable is 3 of those 1249 paths.** ⓘ **CALIBRATION, recorded so the next reader does not over-correct: `.git` is ALREADY 19 GB, so the 1215-PNG churn is ROUTINE at this factory and is NOT what this row is about.** Every full-suite run rewrites every screenshot, PNG is already deflated so git cannot delta it, and that is a large part of how the repo reached 19 GB. **The raw JSON is different in kind** — a single new file, over the hard push limit, that the master explicitly wanted left untracked. **Do not read this row as an argument to prune artifacts**; the RETENTION LAW forbids that independently. 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF: A FIREWALL GOVERNS WHAT A TASK MAY WRITE; IT SAYS NOTHING ABOUT WHAT THE RUNNER WILL COMMIT.** f1643-2’s firewall is impeccable — Touch-ONLY three files, an explicit NO on `reviews/**` and `src/**`, and a FIREWALL LIFT clause ordering the runner to STOP rather than reach outside it. **The runner obeys none of it at commit time**, because `git add -A -- .` is not addressed by any firewall a master can write. The master even anticipates this exact class on the **input** side — the F-1266-1 / F-1407-1 evidence-artifact exceptions (*“`artifacts/**`, `reviews/shots-*` and any `.png` are NEVER work … discard them and PROCEED”*) — **it simply has no output-side counterpart.** ➡️ **So when a task’s byproducts are large or numerous, the firewall is not the control — the DRAIN’s pathspec is. Read the runner’s commit line to predict what a lane branch will carry; the master’s NO list will not tell you.** 🚫 **NO MECHANISM PROPOSED, AND THE REASON IS THE POPULATION.** Widening `lane-runner-v3.sh:284`’s exclusion list is the tempting cure, but that list is a hardcoded enumeration of what git could answer itself — the defect shape this corpus already names — and it would need a fresh entry per task. The other tempting cure, `.gitignore`-ing the raw report, is **INERT for this run and is written down so nobody spends a fire trying it**: the lane worktree checked out its `.gitignore` at `behind=79` and will not see a new one, and its pre-flight ran hours ago. **Editing anything under a live `nice -n 19` run is also precisely the load this hold exists to prevent.** The cure that costs nothing and cannot rot is the drain’s own pathspec — which the house already mandates: *“Path-scoped `git add` only. Never `-A` at repo root.”* ⓘ Nothing here is owed by the owner and nothing is blocked on him.
📏 **F-1670-1 (s1670 2026-08-11, MEASURED ON A DRY BOARD — THE DISPATCH-HOLD IS CORRECT AND I CONFIRMED IT RATHER THAN OVERTURNED IT; BUT THE WINDOW HAS BEEN SIZED IN THE WRONG UNIT SINCE s1666, AND THE RUN STATES ITS OWN DENOMINATOR VERBATIM. NOT AN OWNER QUESTION.)** 📋 **BOARD, VERIFIED NOT INHERITED:** six queues EMPTY · every `tasks/done/` head entry `drained-`/`stopped-` · no `tasks/CODEX-WALL` · assayer `pending/` EMPTY · `authorable-candidates` → **14 planned, 14 priced, 0 fire-authorable** (owner-gated 7 · attended-owed 5 · prose-only 1 · needs-spec 1) · lanes b/c/d `ahead=0` USABLE and idle (55 / 236 / 91 behind) · lane-a **BUSY** throughout. **Nothing to drain, nothing lawful to author, dispatch held — so the budget went to interrogating the one instruction every fire since s1664 has copied forward.** ✅ **THE HOLD SURVIVES ITS OWN AUDIT, AND THAT IS A RESULT, NOT A NON-EVENT.** s1669 had just proven (F-1669-1) that three fires copied a false premise forward; I applied that lesson one layer up and re-derived the dispatch hold at source instead of citing s1669 (B). **It is grounded twice and both grounds hold:** (1) f1643-2's own gate — *"only when all four lanes are idle and no drain is in flight… state the concurrent load it ran under"*; (2) **THE OWNER'S THROTTLE LAW OF TODAY** — verbatim *"can we limit the amount of threads it uses? … I woke up in the middle of the night to it."* → the run carries `--workers=3` + `nice -n 19`. **A confirmed premise is the correct outcome of VERIFY-DON'T-INHERIT at least as often as a refuted one; the discipline is the re-derivation, not the reversal.** 🎯 **THE FINDING — THE SERIAL WINDOW HAS BEEN MEASURED IN FAILURES WHEN THE ARM RUNS EXECUTIONS, AND THE FAN-OUT IS 3.8×.** F-1666-1 correctly identified the second arm (scope 5's `--workers=1` re-run) and correctly ruled that the hold must key on the instrument rather than a clock — but it **sized** that arm at *"451 failures observed at last read."* **The run states its own scope, quoted verbatim from its log at 13:12:33Z:** *"Serial attribution is correctly scoped: **1,706 executions from the 199 failing files**, using exactly one worker. This will be slower than the full pass by design."* ➡️ **Scope 5 re-runs whole failing FILES, not individual failures**, so 451 failures fan out to **1,706 executions across 199 files** — the denominator is **3.8× the number every estimate has been keyed to.** 📊 **MEASURED STATE at 13:38:43Z (20:38 local), from the log's own timestamps:** run started **06:35:10Z**, elapsed **7h08m** · parallel arm **2,804 executions**, 36@06:40:20Z → 2,804@13:10:28Z (**~7.2/min**) · serial arm began ~13:18:10Z, samples **21 → 42 → 70 → 85 → 97 of 1,706**, i.e. **5.7% complete** at **~3.7/min** over its first ~20 min — **roughly half the parallel arm's rate, exactly as the run predicted of itself.** ⚠️ **The rate figure is COARSE and I am labelling it rather than dressing it up: the log carries timestamps only on `codex_models_manager` ERROR lines (676 across 3 MB), so each progress marker is correlated to the nearest PRECEDING stamp and each endpoint carries that gap as error.** The endpoints are sound; the per-interval numbers are not, and one interval (70@13:23:28 vs 42@13:23:14) is arithmetically impossible and is an artefact of exactly this. ⛔ **I PUBLISH NO ETA AND THIS FINDING IS NOT ONE — IT STRENGTHENS F-1666-1 RATHER THAN REPLACING IT.** F-1666-1's ruling stands unchanged and is still the only correct trigger: **dispatch `f1662-2` when `node scripts/lane-usable.mjs lane-a` stops saying BUSY, and not before.** What this row adds is that the clock is off by **more than F-1666-1's own correction assumed** — a fire tempted to re-derive a number should know the remaining arm is of a different ORDER than "nearly done", and should therefore not be tempted at all. 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, and it generalises well past this run: AN ESTIMATE IS ONLY AS GOOD AS ITS UNIT. When a phase re-runs a coarser CONTAINER than the thing being counted — files containing failures, suites containing tests, batches containing items — the denominator FANS OUT by a factor nobody wrote down.** F-1666-1 did the hard part right (it found the hidden phase) and still under-stated it, because it sized the phase in the unit it had (`failures`) rather than the unit the phase consumes (`executions`). **Before projecting from `x/N`, prove `N` counts the same unit as the work — and prefer the artifact's own statement of its scope over any count you derive yourself.** 🪤 **TRAP-MARKER, hit by me this fire and cheap to disarm: `ls -l` PRINTS LOCAL TIME AND `statSync().mtime.toISOString()` PRINTS UTC, AND THIS REPO'S CLOCK IS UTC+07.** The same live write reads `Aug 11 20:33` from `ls -l` and `2026-08-11T13:33:40.449Z` from node — **one instant, two labels seven hours apart.** The log's internal `codex` stamps are true UTC. **A fire that compares an `ls -l` mtime against the log's own ERROR timestamps will conclude a 7-hour stall on a run that wrote one minute ago**, and on this board that mis-reading would license exactly the dispatch the throttle law forbids. It is the §1.1 stamp hazard wearing a different hat: **judge liveness by comparing like with like, and convert before you subtract.** ⓘ Nothing here is owed by the owner and nothing is blocked on him.
🪦 **F-1669-1 (s1669 2026-08-11, CAUGHT BY REFUSING TO INHERIT A NEXT-FIRE INSTRUCTION — THREE CONSECUTIVE FIRES ORDERED THEIR SUCCESSOR TO PERFORM A PROCEDURE ON A BLOCK THAT WAS LIFTED SEVEN FIRES AGO. THE FALSE PREMISE CAME FROM A FILENAME, WHICH IS A FOSSIL THE LEDGER CAN REVERSE AND NOTHING RENAMES. NOT AN OWNER QUESTION.)** 📋 **HOW IT SURFACED:** the board was dry again (six queues EMPTY, no `CODEX-WALL`, assayer `pending/` EMPTY, `tasks/done/` head all `drained-`/`stopped-`), lane-a still **BUSY**, so priority (B) applied and the budget went to verifying what I inherited. s1668's line-1 (A) reads verbatim: *"drain the f1643-2 output first (it is a `gate-side` hold, so §3.0's STOP is on the MERGE only; gather evidence in a detached worktree, then **flip the leaf out of `blocked`** in a SECOND commit right after the code commit, F-1384-1)"*. I ran §3.0's own first command against it. `node scripts/drain-block-check.mjs stopped-s1655-gate-side-…-f1643-2-suite-red-inventory-refresh.md` → **`✅ CLEAR — … status="queued"`**. **There is no block, no `blockClass`, and nothing to flip.** 🎯 **ROOT CAUSE — A DONE-MOVE FILENAME IS A CLOSURE CLAIM MINTED ONCE AND NEVER RENAMED.** s1665 introduced the claim and its own text names the evidence it used: *"it is a `gate-side` hold (`stopped-s1655-gate-side-…`)"* — **the filename**. That string was minted by s1655 at 11:42:59 when the leaf genuinely was `blocked`/`gate-side`. **s1657 `9a32d94f2` (2026-08-11T13:34:51+07:00) then lifted it** — the diff deletes `"status":"blocked"`, deletes `"blockClass":"gate-side"`, deletes `blockedReason`, writes `"status":"queued"` and **dispatches the master to lane-a**. That dispatch is the run still live as I write (`tasks/runs/20260811-133510-lane-a-…f1643-2….log`, lane-a tracked-dirt 1205 and climbing). **The ledger reversed the state 1h52m after the filename froze, and the filename cannot say so.** s1665 → s1667 → s1668 each re-copied the instruction forward; s1667's own header says *"UNCHANGED FROM s1666 BECAUSE THE BLOCKER IS UNCHANGED"*, which was true of the LOAD hold and false of the class. ⚠️ **THE LIVE HAZARD IS WORSE THAN A WASTED PARAGRAPH, AND IT IS ARMED FOR THE NEXT FIRE THAT SEES LANE-A GO IDLE.** The stopped run this filename names **left zero diff** — its own preserved block reason says so verbatim: *"ended in ~4 minutes without launching the day-scale suite… and it left zero diff"*. A fire obeying (A) literally goes to `tasks/done/stopped-s1655-…` as its gating subject and gates **a run that changed nothing** — Mistake #1, the Silent No-Op, arriving by INHERITANCE rather than from a runner. The real drain target when lane-a frees is the **13:35 run's** output, and its bookkeeping is an ordinary `queued` → `merged` flip, not an F-1384-1 two-commit block-lift. 📊 **THE CLASS IS REAL AND MEASURED, NOT A ONE-OFF:** of **1270** files in `tasks/done/` (283 `drained-`, 72 `stopped-`, 915 other), **6** carry a closure-class token in the filename; 5 resolve to a leaf, and **2 of those 5 (40%) have been REVERSED by the ledger** — this one, and `stopped-lawful-s1176-blocked-by-26-cell-master-divergence-…-lane-c-eight-winds-wiring-hero.md`, whose leaf reads **`status:"merged"` at `29bac3d9478e9f3a77385c75965b60447a90500c`**. The other 3 still read `⛔ CLOSED — DO NOT DRAIN` and their filenames remain honest. **A filename records the state at done-move time; only the ledger records the state now.** 🚫 **NO MECHANISM PROPOSED, AND THE REASON IS THE USEFUL HALF — THE OBVIOUS CURE WOULD NOT HAVE PREVENTED THIS.** The tempting fix is to have `drain-block-check` print the disagreement when a filename's class token contradicts the live leaf; it is cheap, purely mechanical (string vs field, no judgement), and it would have been **useless here, because s1665 never ran the tool.** It held the filename, read its words, and wrote them into an instruction. **A tool only ever helps the fire that asks it** — the same structural gap F-1582-1 named when it declined