🔓 **F-1668-1 (s1668 2026-08-11, MEASURED WHILE RE-PROVING A BANKED MASTER’S DISPATCH PRECONDITIONS — THE FIFTH PRE-FLIGHT KEY OF `f1662-2` IS SELF-ROTTING, AND THE FIRE THAT VERIFIED IT IS THE FIRE THAT BROKE IT. CURED IN THE MASTER. NOT AN OWNER QUESTION.)** 📋 **HOW IT SURFACED:** the board was dry (six queues empty, no undrained done-move, no `CODEX-WALL`, assayer empty, `authorable-candidates` → 14 planned / 14 priced / **0 fire-authorable**), lane-a still **BUSY**, so the one lawful act available was to re-prove the banked master’s dispatch preconditions rather than inherit them (Mistake #4). Four keys returned **1**. The fifth returned **2**. 🎯 **ROOT CAUSE — THE KEY’S CORPUS IS A LEDGER EVERY FIRE WRITES TO.** The fifth key is a *freshness probe*: `grep -c` of an `F-1662-2 MASTER AUTHORED` + authoring-session string against **`tasks/BACKLOG.md`**, whose purpose is to prove the lane carries the commit that authored the master (F-1424-3). But s1666 measured all five keys at exactly 1, then wrote the **F-1666-1** row asserting it — and **that row’s own backticked quotation of the key string took the count to 2** (`tasks/BACKLOG.md:5` alongside the original at `:9`, re-measured s1668). **Verifying the key is what broke the key.** The authoring fire did nothing wrong; a row that cites its evidence by content is exactly what the house style demands. ⚠️ **WHY IT IS A LIVE DISPATCH HAZARD AND NOT A CURIOSITY:** the master’s block says `# expect 1` and its prose says *“If any returns 0, **STOP** and report ‘lane is stale or the cited code moved’”*. The literal STOP predicate is `0`, so a strictly-literal runner proceeds — but the runner is *told to expect 1* and sees 2, and the house rule is that a runner reporting an adjacent problem is **good behaviour**. The likely outcome is a STOP whose error text accuses **the wrong subject** (a stale lane), sending the next fire chasing a phantom refresh. That is the F-1424-3 shape exactly: a pre-flight mismatch that costs a whole dispatch (44,007 tokens, zero files) while the lane is in fact perfectly fresh. **And it only grows** — every future fire that records this master’s dispatch in BACKLOG adds another. ➡️ **CURED IN THE MASTER, AT THE PREDICATE RATHER THAN THE COUNT:** `tasks/f1662-2-audit-exemption-count.md` now marks the fifth key `# expect >= 1` with a note under the block stating why a count above 1 is **not** a defect and must not be a STOP, and the lead-in sentence flags that it has already risen to 2 by lawful ledger-keeping. **The first four keys keep `== 1` deliberately** — their corpora are source files with a single site each (`scripts/same-game-audit.mjs`, `src/sim/HeadlessContractSim.ts`), which no fire’s bookkeeping touches. The fix is one line of predicate plus the reason; the master is otherwise untouched and stays dispatch-ready. 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, AND IT AMENDS F-1425-2 RATHER THAN RESTATING IT:** F-1425-2 says *prove the citation key against the source file on main before writing it down*, because a paraphrase matches nowhere. **That is necessary and it is not sufficient.** It says nothing about what happens to the key AFTERWARDS — and if the key’s corpus is a file the factory writes to (BACKLOG, STATUS, a ledger), then **the act of recording the verification mutates the thing verified.** s1666 obeyed F-1425-2 perfectly and still handed the next fire a broken key. ⚙️ **THE RULE THAT FALLS OUT, cheap and needing no mechanism: a citation key’s predicate must match its corpus’s VOLATILITY.** Keys anchored in **source** ask `== 1` (a second hit means the code really did move). Keys anchored in a **ledger** ask `>= 1`, because their real question is *presence*, and presence is the only property a growing corpus preserves. **This is the F-1300-4 class with the subject swapped** — there, a fire’s late bookkeeping invalidates a guard’s earlier green; here, a fire’s bookkeeping invalidates its own earlier *measurement*. Both come from the same structural fact: **a fire is a writer of the corpus it is measuring.** 🚫 **NO GUARD PROPOSED, AND THE REASON IS THE POPULATION, NOT A PREFERENCE.** A checker would have to decide which corpora are volatile and which keys are freshness probes rather than code anchors — a judgement, and the corpus already records what happens to guards built on judgements: they fire on legitimate cases and get excused into uselessness (`cross-engine`, F-1460-1; the same call s1665 and s1667 each made). The live population is **one key in one banked master**, now cured. **If a second ledger-anchored key ever appears in a master, that is the signal to reconsider — and this row is the baseline.**
