nd this is an addition. ➡️ **THE CHEAP RULE, WHICH IS THE WHOLE DELIVERABLE — WHEN WRITING THE DESK, KEEP THE HEADER'S PROSE FREE OF BOTH A LITERAL 🔺 AND ANY F-ID.** Say *"one marker per item"*, never *"one 🔺 each"*; and put any commentary that names an id (e.g. *"the cheapest unblock is F-1656-3"*) **after the last item**, where its segment already has an owner and the trailing id is ignored. Costs nothing, needs no mechanism. 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF IS ABOUT THE LAW, NOT THE PARSER: §4 orders a fire to run `desk-state-audit --status` on what it INHERITED — the previous fire's archived line-1 — and nothing orders it to run the reader over the desk it is about to WRITE.** That asymmetry is why both mechanisms of this class were minted by a fire that had just correctly obeyed §4: the read-side duty is instrumented, the write-side one is not, so every desk defect is discovered one fire late by its victim instead of one minute early by its author. **The one-line habit that closes it: after writing line-1 and before committing, run the reader on `STATUS.md` and check the number against your own header.** I did, which is the only reason this row is a note and not an incident.
