✅ **F-1608-1 (s1608 2026-08-10 — THE GZ-01 SWEEP RE-PRICED AND RUN; AND A SECOND BLIND SPOT FOUND IN THE SAME CHECK, OF EXACTLY F-1600-1's CLASS).** F-1600-1 ordered the backfill sweep re-priced before anyone worked from its raw figure. Done, with the check widened in three ways and then RUN: `scripts/gazette-backfill-sweep.mjs` (new, advisory, exit 0 always — F-1600-1 forbids mechanising this as a red guard, since "player-visible" is a judgement and such a guard gets excused into uselessness within a week, the `cross-engine` fate). It (1) greps the **whole of `marketing/outbox/`**, not `gazette-queue.md` alone, because the TK-01 ticker digests are a second owner-facing sink; (2) indexes citations **verbatim and tests by PREFIX** rather than by a fixed 8-char slice — an earlier draft of my own script could not have matched a 7-char citation, the same shape of blindness in miniature; and (3) also tests every commit a merge **INTRODUCED**, because the existing roundups cite lane-side hashes, so keying on the merge hash alone is a matcher narrower than the citation convention. 📊 **MEASURED since 2026-08-05: 68 player-path-touching first-parent merges · 60 already cited in one sink or the other · 8 candidates.** Widening (3) changed the count by **zero** — a real negative result, not a skipped check: all 60 are cited by their own merge hash, so the answer is stable under the wider key. ⚖️ **THE 8 THEN JUDGED ONE AT A TIME, WHICH IS THE PART NO SCRIPT CAN DO: 3 genuinely owed · 1 ALREADY REPORTED · 4 not player-visible** (a Python verifier under `assets/pilots/`, a `\u0000` escaping fix with no behaviour, ledger bookkeeping, and a headless-door de-list — the first is a false positive of the path filter, which counts `assets/` as a player path). 🔑 **THE NEW BLIND SPOT, AND IT IS THE EXPENSIVE ONE: A FEATURE THAT LANDS ACROSS TWO MERGES AND IS ANNOUNCED ONCE LEAVES THE FIRST MERGE PERMANENTLY "ABSENT".** `ba78dad5e` (2026-08-05, the rider `stack` metadata + the Field Book reader) reads as never-reported, and it is not: the county-board GAZETTE item published its player-facing outcome on 2026-08-08 under the LATER hash `964978bad` — *"Every entry now names the mind and the rig it declared"*. **The duty is to report a CHANGE; the check is keyed on HASHES**, and those are not the same thing whenever a feature arc spans merges. This is precisely the harm F-1600-1 named — *"a check that reports a discharged duty as owed reports it forever, and every fire pays the judgement cost again"* — surviving in a new place after that finding's own cure. ⓘ **NO further mechanism proposed, deliberately:** the only reliable discriminator is reading the item and the diff, which is what a judging fire already does. The cure is this row, so the next sweeper does not re-open `ba78dad5e` a third time. **GATE: none — sweep run, items written, nothing owed.** 📮 **WRITTEN: one GAZETTE + one ROUNDUP** in `marketing/outbox/gazette-queue.md`. Fort solidity (`219ba70d1`) was **broken out separately because it carries a LIVE OWNER CHOICE** — whether landmark forts may ever be damaged or besieged, with the wrecker's gap-flow bypass waiting on the same word — and the law says an owner choice is the one thing a roundup must never bury. The far ground (`f065907c7`) and the living paper (`16dff1dc5`) were batched into one ROUNDUP per the ≤3/week rule. 😳 **AND I COMMITTED THE VERY DEFECT I WAS WRITING ABOUT, WHICH IS WORTH ONE LINE BECAUSE THE NEXT FIRE TO DESCRIBE IT WILL BE AT THE SAME RISK.** Writing the phrase "a `\u0000` escaping fix" into this row wrote a **literal NUL byte** into `tasks/BACKLOG.md` — the exact fault `1a2871fd3` cured in `Game.ts`, and the reason that merge exists (a NUL makes `grep` treat the file as binary, so it reports **nothing**, not an error). It was caught within a minute only because my own `grep -n "escaping fix"` returned empty when it plainly should not have — **an unexplained negative result is a signal, not a nuisance** — and `scripts/nul-audit.mjs` then named it at offset 2921. Cured by constructing the NUL with `String.fromCharCode(0)` in a fix script rather than typing one (a file that types the character to fix the character re-introduces it); `nul-audit` now reads **CLEAN**. ⓘ The guard already existed and would have reddened at `test:ledger-guards`, my last act — so the mechanism worked; what nearly failed was noticing an hour earlier. ⚠️ **THEN IT HAPPENED A SECOND TIME, IN THE COMMIT WRITING THIS VERY WARNING** — quoting the offending phrase back into the row to explain it re-wrote the byte, and `nul-audit` caught it again within the minute. **That is the actual lesson, and it is not carelessness: any prose that QUOTES the character re-introduces it, so the hazard is a property of writing about NULs at all.** ➡️ Therefore: never paste the literal to describe it — write the six-character escape, and run `node scripts/nul-audit.mjs` after any ledger edit that so much as mentions delimiters.

