ut — it runs `( cd "$wd" && git add -A -- . … && git commit … )`, i.e. onto **whatever branch the worktree is sitting on**. Codex executes the pre-flight prose itself, so the reset moves the worktree off `lane/d` BEFORE the runner commits: the run lands on `lane/perf` while the done-move says `lane-d`, and the next drain reads `lane/d` at `ahead=0` and concludes the work never happened — **the vanished-casualty shape, arriving through the front door.** ⚠️ **AND IT COMPOUNDS: both LANE-D masters reset the SAME wrong branch**, so whichever ran second would `-B` force-reset it back to main **over the first's undrained output** — the F-1522-1 / Reset Massacre shape. The runner's own LANE-SAFETY probe cannot see any of it: it resolves the slot's branch correctly *before* dispatch, and Codex switches away *after*. ✅ **CURED PRE-DISPATCH** (4 single-token edits, one occurrence each, verified before writing), and **the rest of the batch re-verified and found SOUND** — I opened every READ-FIRST coordinate in all four against main and **all ten are ACCURATE** (`gr-sim.mjs:62` the ceiling / `:72` the throw · `HeadlessContractSim.ts:73-86` exactly `bossKillSecuresRun` · `skill.md:34` the fails-closed sentence · `Game.ts:5048-5056` `nightSpeedMultiplier` · `HeadlessContractSim.ts:313-347` the mothSeason-only LightField · `LockstepClient.ts:250` · `_multiplayer.ts:270` · `buildables.ts:204-210` the round-up-to-5 curves · `MechanicsManifest.ts:399` the lone `costCurve(0)` · `agent-view.spec.ts:265`). **One defect class, four instances, nothing else wrong — the batch is good work with one copy-paste line in it.** 🧬 **CLASS, NOT SLIP: 280 of 942 masters carry a slot/branch mismatch.** ⚠️ **That 280 is NOT a defect backlog and must NOT be "cured"** — nearly all are historical SHIPPED masters that were CORRECT when authored under the retired mapping (lane-a=`lane/m3`, lane-b=`lane/m4`, lane-c=`lane/e2-arsenal`|`lane/polish`, lane-d=`lane/perf`) that F-1464-3 measured false; the rest are `<lane-branch>` placeholders. But they are **280 readable exemplars**, so every master drafted from an old one inherits the stale line — which is exactly what happened, **twice, in two attended commits 52 minutes apart**, by a session that had `git worktree list` available the whole time. 🔎 **F-1539-2 — THE GUARD FOR THIS CLASS ALREADY EXISTED AND WAS GREEN BECAUSE IT WAS LOOKING AT NOTHING.** Before building a mechanism I checked for one (*the SAME FILE may already solve it*): `scripts/banked-master-preflight-guard.test.mjs`, shipped s1408, already in `test:ledger-guards`, same shape by name and intent, and it had already solved the hard part — refusing to sweep all 942 masters. ⚠️ **But its denominator was `status:"queued"` ALONE, and that status is TRANSIENT** — a leaf carries it only from the moment a fire `cp`s the master into a queue slot until the runner claims it, **about a minute**. A master authored and waiting for a refill — **the literal meaning of "banked", the entire population the guard is named for** — is `planned`. 📊 **Measured: `queued` = 0 leaves board-wide** (of 683: merged 575, shipped 52, superseded 20, planned 13, verified-by-owner 11, blocked 6, building 3, stopped 3). **The test ran, examined NOTHING, and reported green** while the four defective masters sat on disk. 💡 **THE LESSON, worth more than its trigger: a guard is not green because the tree is clean, it is green because it LOOKED — and nothing in a passing test tells the two apart. A denominator keyed on a transient state is empty whenever the work is WAITING rather than MOVING**, and waiting is exactly when a banked-master guard has a job. `git log -S` confirms `queued` is genuinely used, so this was not dead vocabulary and s1408 was not careless — the status simply cannot be observed at rest. ✅ **CURED: denominator widened to `planned`+`queued`, reasoning written at the definition so it is not narrowed back by accident; the s1408 test is still green on a denominator that is no longer empty** (all four carry the F-1407-1 churn exception). F-1539-1's guard landed in the **same file**, reusing the corrected denominator — **no new npm script, so no gate-rooting question.** ✅ **RED PATH PROVEN BY REPLAYING THE REAL INCIDENT, NOT A MOCK** (the s1299/s1300 standard): restoring the four masters from `29d560c61` reddened it naming **all four with their exact wrong branches**, while the sibling s1408 test stayed green — the two are independent. Probe reverted; all four verified **byte-identical by blob hash**. ✅ **s1538 NEXT(D) DISCHARGED ON ITS OWN TERMS.** Its gate was explicit — *"a fire that has just run the battery for another reason folds its real counts into the law in the same commit — do NOT run it solely to count."* I ran it to verify the F-1539-2 guard, so the gate was met, and `fire.md:23` now carries **RE-MEASURED s1539: 13 node test files / 97 tests (97 pass, 0 fail, 15.3 s) + 11 chained leaves**, against the stale *"eleven guards / 12 leaf files / 38 tests"*. The old figures are **RESTATED, NOT DELETED** — the drift is the provenance (~2.5× growth in tests, nothing decayed), and the cheapness claim is re-stated honestly: this is still the **seconds** battery; `test:node-guards` is the 181 s one. Written as a **SAME-LINE edit** (47 lines before and after) so no coordinate shifted; `law-pointer-guard` **11/11 green** after. 🧪 **GATES (law surfaces + ledger rows are late-mutated subjects, so the battery ran AFTER the bookkeeping, per s1301):** the 13 node ledger guards **97/97 pass, 0 fail**; all 11 chained leaves green — `findings-state` 0 double-state · `blocker-panel` 0 closed-on-panel · `ruling-propagation` 0 stale · `citations` PASS · `attended-owed` 1 open (attended-side, not a defect) · `main-lock-gate` 7 fixtures · `janitor-rejection` · `lane-dispatch-safety` · `nul-audit` CLEAN. `desk-declaration` SKIPped mid-fire under my own ACTIVE lock — **recorded as a SKIP, not a pass**; it gates for real at handoff. ⓘ `status-archive-audit