/Users/robin/Obsidian/Brain/Projects/Gold Rush/Agent seat — a headless sim can ride a lockstep room, but the two engines fingerprint the world differently — 2026-08-06.md:20:**1. A relay rate limit is a tick-rate law.** The relay allows 600 messages / 10 s per rider; a seat spends one `input` per tick. Unthrottled, a headless sim (hundreds of times faster than realtime) gets ejected for `rate_limited`. Default 30 ticks/s, ceiling 45. Lockstep *would* pace the seat eventually — but only after the inputs are sent, which is the traffic being limited, so **the throttle has to sit in front of the wire, not behind it**. The ceiling is now read out of `functions/api/_multiplayer.ts` at test time so it reds instead of rotting.
/Users/robin/Obsidian/Brain/Projects/Gold Rush/Agent seat — a headless sim can ride a lockstep room, but the two engines fingerprint the world differently — 2026-08-06.md-21-
/Users/robin/Obsidian/Brain/Projects/Gold Rush/Agent seat — a headless sim can ride a lockstep room, but the two engines fingerprint the world differently — 2026-08-06.md:22:**2. Two engines can both be honest and still never agree on a hash.** `Game.multiplayerStateHash` hashes a *run-suspend snapshot*; `HeadlessContractSim.tickHash` hashes its own *planar state*. Same world, different fingerprint — so a human + rig room mismatches at **tick 0 by construction**, and the seat resigns naming its engine (`gr-sim.headless.v1`) so the mismatch reads as an engine difference rather than corruption. **Seat ↔ seat plays a shared run; human ↔ seat opens the door and then stops.** There is a screenshot of a rig sitting at a human's table (the host's RIDERS panel reads "Robin of Dawn Claim" + "Rig of Calculating House"), and the 390px capture catches the game calling the rig's exit *"Holding the trail — Rig has 60 seconds to return"*: it already has vocabulary for a rider leaving.
